    "result_text": "The change is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**What changed** (`toE164`): after a candidate number passes the general E.164 shape check (`+` followed by 8\u201315 digits), it now also validates the *national* number length against its country code via a new helper:\n\n```java\nprivate static boolean hasValidNationalLength(String digits) {\n    if (digits.startsWith(\"61\")) {\n        return digits.length() - 2 == 9;   // +61 (AU) => 9 national digits\n    }\n    if (digits.startsWith(\"1\")) {\n        return digits.length() - 1 == 10;  // +1 (NANP) => 10 national digits\n    }\n    return true;                            // other codes: general 8\u201315 rule only\n}\n```\n\nWhen the length is wrong for the country, `toE164` returns `null`, which causes `normalizeTelephone` to throw `InvalidFieldValueException` \u2014 mapped by the exception advice to **400 Bad Request**, exactly as the spec requires.\n\n**Design notes:**\n- Folding the check into `toE164` keeps the \"is this a valid E.164 number\" gate in one place. It's also used for duplicate comparison, where the effect is only to safely ignore malformed stored numbers.\n- Only the two specified country codes (`+61`, `+1`) are pinned; other codes fall back to the general rule. The prefixes `1` and `61` don't overlap, so classification is unambiguous.\n- The pre-existing `uniqueTelephone()` generator (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still satisfies the new rule, so no other tests regressed.\n\nTest results: `AcceptanceTest` and the full suite \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
